# Hostile audit — finite-defect KKT strip through `t<=1.86596`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:**
- `research/target_c_finite_defect_kkt_strip_t186596_2026-09-15.md`
- `research/verify_target_c_finite_defect_kkt_strip_t186596.py`

**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only full-core Type-II scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The candidate transports the finite-defect averaging argument from the current
`1.8623` coupled path to the much thinner paired-remainder O--O path proved through
`1.86596`. No new local geometry is introduced. The audit checks that all three global
ledgers and the path-region assignment survive this change of path source.

## 1. The `1.86596` family has the required path structure

The hostile-audited paired-remainder endpoint theorem supplies more than

\[
(17/10^6)D-o(D)
\]

actual complete O--O trains in one dominant phase window. Their opposite oriented
terminal branches lie in two disjoint boundary arcs. The Jordan crosscut theorem makes
their proper midpoint crosscuts pairwise noncrossing, hence one simple complement-dual
path. The same theorem stack supplies phase-free region saturation and the common
Helly routing region.

These are exactly the topological inputs used by the `1.8623` finite-defect argument.
No center-fan, small-leaf, or low-`t` rectangle hypothesis is imported.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 2. Signed diameter incidence is still one constant-sign interval

Laminar rooted shores depend only on the ordered noncrossing O--O crosscuts. For one
actual diameter edge, the set of path cuts it crosses is therefore one interval; while
inside that interval the same endpoint remains on the rooted shore, so the sign cannot
flip. Thus each actual diameter row changes at most twice, independently of how the
O--O path was extracted. Hence both

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2|H|
\]

and

\[
\sum_iB_i\le2\sum_h\beta_h=2
\]

remain exact.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. Diameter count bound is source-global

Hopf--Pannwitz gives `|H|<=q`. Consecutive loaded force-hull vertices are source points
and hence their hull-side lengths are at least one, so `q<=per(F)`. Cauchy's
perimeter-diameter inequality gives `per(F)<=pi D`. Therefore

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2\pi D
\]

without any `t<=1.8623` input.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. The stopped-train terminal ledger is uniform through `T=1.86596`

The exact identities

\[
T_{tr}=(b+Q)/2+2f,
\qquad b+Q=2(q-k)
\]

give `T_tr=tD+2f`. Every bounded nonquadrilateral face contributes at least one to
`Q`, so `f<=Q`. The perimeter ledger

\[
Q/D\le2T-\pi+o(1)
\]

is uniform for every source with `t<=T`; it is not tied to the previous endpoint.
Thus

\[
2T_{tr}/D\le10T-4\pi+o(1)
\]

is valid at `T=1.86596`. The exact verifier checks the adverse endpoint using an
outward lower bound for `pi`.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Terminal occurrences can be assigned to the new path regions

A selected O--O crosscut is the midpoint path of one complete stopped train. Its
interior runs through strict rhombi; its endpoints lie on the outer face. It does not
enter the interior of a bounded nonquadrilateral face. Therefore each bounded
exceptional face lies wholly in one complement region of the selected path and all
terminal face-side occurrences on that face can be assigned to that region.

Outer-face terminal occurrences lie on one boundary subarc of one path region. Endpoint
ties involve only selected cut endpoints and can be assigned arbitrarily without
increasing the total count. Hence

\[
\sum_iL_i\le2T_{tr}
\]

for this path exactly as for the coupled path.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. Joint average and integer consequences are correctly rounded

The verifier proves with exact Machin arithmetic

\[
{10T-2\pi+2\over17/10^6}<845672.
\]

Deleting the hub region and two end regions changes a linear path denominator by
`o(D)`. Therefore for all sufficiently large sources one eligible region has

\[
N_i+L_i+D B_i<845675.
\]

Because `N_i,L_i` are nonnegative integers,

\[
N_i,L_i\le845674.
\]

Every bounded exceptional face has degree at least five and all its terminal
occurrences are counted in `L_i`; therefore its number is at most

\[
\lfloor845674/5\rfloor=169134.
\]

All displayed integer constants are safe.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 7. Crossing-neighborhood symmetric difference is charged injectively

If a complete train crosses `tau_i` but not `tau_{i+1}`, it enters the intervening
region at its unique strict-rhombus crossing with `tau_i`. Distinct complete trains
meet at most once, so it cannot return across `tau_i`; by hypothesis it does not leave
through `tau_{i+1}`. As a stopped path it must terminate in the region, consuming one
assigned terminal occurrence. Different defect trains have different train-terminal
occurrences. The reverse set difference is identical.

Thus

\[
|\Gamma(\tau_i)\triangle\Gamma(\tau_{i+1})|\le L_i.
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 8. KKT/resultant conclusions use the same positive stress

The relative-interior KKT normalization has `sum beta_h=1` and all actual active rows
positive. For Section-157 resultants

\[
F_j=\sum_h\beta_h\varepsilon_{hj}r_h,
\qquad |r_h|=D,
\]

the triangle inequality gives

\[
\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|\le D B_i,
\]

while reverse triangle inequality gives the same bound on `|A_{i+1}-A_i|`. The
adjacent-strip reaction nondegeneracy theorem is path-local and therefore applies to
this selected nonhub region. Planar conic Caratheodory reduces its nonzero reaction to
one or two actual switching diameter bars.

No uniform lower bound on an individual `beta_h` is inferred from the small total
switching mass.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 9. Scope

The theorem gives fixed complexity, not closure. `845675` is deliberately crude and
the two reaction bars may carry arbitrarily small KKT weights. The result does not
supply an `O(1/D)` phase gap or bounded Euclidean blocker clusters, and it does not
address the separate Type-I macroscopic-container branch.

**Final verdict:** **PASS.** The finite-defect, low-KKT, one-sided nonhub strip normal
form is valid throughout the paired-remainder range `t<=1.86596`.
